### Fix 1 — `ShellJobRegistry` zone capture (root cause)
Capture the construction-time zone and run the settle continuation inside it:

```dart
/// The zone the host constructed this registry in (boot). Job settle
/// callbacks (and the host's onSetled — which starts model-facing
/// reaction runs) must fire there, NEVER in the zone of the bash call
/// that happened to start the job (gh-1947): a job started by a child
/// used to carry its settle into the child's subagent zone, so a later
/// settle-wake ran the MAIN agent's reaction run with
/// `activeSubagentId()` still naming the finished child — every
/// schedule_message in it misrouted to the child's mailbox.
final Zone _bootZone;
``
In constructor: `_bootZone = Zone.current;` (needs `dart:async` import — check). In start():
```dart
unawaited(
 _bootZone.run(() => entry.setled.then((_) async { ... })),
);
``

Wait — `.then` inside `_bootZone.run` — the closure passed to run executes synchronously in `_bootZone`; the `.then` REGISTRATION binds the listener to `_bootZone` ✓. The callback body — including `onSetled?.call(entry)` — runs in `_bootZone` ✓.

Also bind `onStart?.call(entry)`? It's called synchronously in start() — the caller's zone — CLI's onStart → job board push (`_jobBoard.start`?) — bookeeping only, no runs. Bind anyway for symmetry? Minimal change is better; but consider `onStart` → `_pushJobBoard`... no run starts. Leave.

Check: does shell_jobs.dart import dart:async already? (uses Timer/unawaited — yes surely).

### Fix 2 — defensive recipient resolution in the CLI + app
CLI `_childSenderMailbox`:
```dart
String? _childSenderMailbox() {
 final id = activeSubagentId();
 if (id == null) return null;
 // gh-1947 guard: the zone can outlive the child's run (a settle
 // continuation started this very tool call inside a finished child's
 // scope). Trust the scope only while the child is genuinely in flight;
 // otherwise the caller is MAIN and its self-reminders must default to
 // MAIN's mailbox.
 final executor = _taskConfig.executor;
 if (!executor.isInFlight(id)) return null;
